23
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:18,200
which is why he's writing about it, why I made it into the title.

24
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:24,140
So as the application is processing a request, it transfers between different states.

25
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:28,220
And abusing those states is what makes the race condition.

26
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:37,860
So he has a really great example here of a race condition, which is not a particularly hard to reproduce race condition

27
00:02:37,860 --> 00:02:40,340
or a particularly technically challenging race condition.

28
00:02:40,940 --> 00:02:49,480
But essentially, when you're signing up for an account, there would be a post to, this is an example he has here,

29
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:56,480
a post to slash login, and then a post to, or a get to slash role.

30
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:01,040
And he kind of maps out here the different states that the machine goes through.

31
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:08,220
When you, you know, your login state, when before you do slash post to slash login is null.

32
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:15,740
And then after you have logged in, you assume that because it redirects you directly to slash role,

33
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:18,540
that, you know, your account goes into a pending state.

34
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:19,740
That's what it looks like on the screen.

35
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:21,540
That's what the user makes the assumption off of.

36
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:25,920
And then after you select your role, it does a post to slash role and assigns you the role.

37
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:35,200
However, you can only be assigned the role that you, you know, are allowed to select from the list on the slash get or on the slash role page.

38
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:44,560
And so that's the way that that this sort of state machine flow went according to, you know, the natural progression of our thought.

39
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:53,960
However, he noticed that when you are able to intercept the application before you hit slash role,

40
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:57,860
by default, your account gets assigned admin permissions.

41
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:03,080
And this is an example of the state machine doing something a little bit funky.

42
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:06,260
And this is a multi-step race condition.

43
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:10,800
You know, there's, if we just pause before slash role and then do a request somewhere else,

44
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:12,760
then we have admin permissions and that's great.

45
00:04:14,220 --> 00:04:18,020
But, but, so this is a multi-step.

46
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:19,100
This takes multiple requests.

47
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:29,760
But the assumption then that he explores within the rest of the paper is that there are these sort of substates that happen within the processing of a single HTTP request.

48
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:35,900
And then how can we abuse these substates to get us access to crazy cool functionality?

